Q8. RTC-BASED ACCESS EVENT LOGGER
====================================================================

Scenario:
Use the onboard User button as an access-event request. Every valid press
must receive an RTC timestamp and be stored in RAM. The LEDs show activity;
the SWD debugger is used to inspect the stored records. No display or UART
terminal is required.

Tasks:
1. Configure the RTC with an initial date and time using an internal clock
   source such as LSI, so no external crystal or component is required.
2. Keep PD12 ON in the ready state.
3. Configure PA0 as an EXTI input and apply software debounce so one physical
   press creates one event.
4. For each valid press, increment an event counter, read the RTC date and
   time, store the record in a RAM ring buffer, and blink PD13 for 500 ms.
5. Store at least the five most recent event records. Each record must contain
   an event number and date/time fields.
6. When the event count is a multiple of five, blink PD15 three times and
   expose the five records for inspection in the debugger.
7. Use PD14 for an invalid RTC or state error, and document how the error is
   cleared.
8. Test normal presses, a held button, rapid presses, event numbers 5 and 10,
   and RTC rollover at a minute boundary.
9. Keep interrupt handlers short and keep SWD enabled.

====================================================================
Q9. DMA-DRIVEN ONBOARD LED PATTERN PLAYER
====================================================================

Scenario:
Play a repeating LED pattern from memory without repeatedly writing the GPIO
register from the main loop. A timer update event must request DMA transfers
to the GPIOD bit set/reset register (BSRR).

Tasks:
1. Create a Flash or RAM table containing a repeating sequence of LED masks.
2. Configure a timer update event at 5 Hz or another documented rate.
3. Configure DMA in circular memory-to-peripheral mode with source increment
   enabled and the GPIOD BSRR destination fixed.
4. Build each 32-bit BSRR table value so that the four previous LEDs are
   cleared and the desired PD12-PD15 pattern is set.
5. Start the timer and DMA. The main loop must not write the LED pattern on
   every step.
6. Verify that the LEDs repeat the pattern and that the DMA buffer index,
   transfer-complete status and timer update event change as expected.
7. Add a PA0-controlled pause/resume feature without stopping the debugger
   connection.
8. Test an all-OFF pattern, an all-ON pattern, a single-LED pattern and a
   repeated pattern with consecutive identical entries.

====================================================================
Q10. WATCHDOG FAULT-RECOVERY CONTROLLER
====================================================================

Scenario:
Use the Independent Watchdog to recover the system if the main application
stops operating. The onboard LEDs must show normal operation and the reason
for a watchdog reset.

Tasks:
1. Configure the IWDG using the internal LSI clock. Select a documented
   timeout, such as approximately 2 seconds, using the actual or measured LSI
   frequency.
2. Use a periodic timer interrupt to set a heartbeat flag. The main loop may
   refresh the watchdog only when the heartbeat and all required application
   health checks are valid.
3. Keep PD12 ON during healthy operation and periodically toggle PD13 as a
   live-operation indicator.
4. If PA0 is held for at least 3 seconds, enter a deliberate test-fault state
   that stops watchdog refresh. Do not use an external fault switch.
5. On the next startup, inspect the RCC reset flags. If the previous reset
   was caused by IWDG, keep PD14 ON for 3 seconds, then return to normal.
6. Clear the reset flags after recording the cause and expose the reset-cause
   variable to the SWD debugger.
7. Confirm that a healthy application does not reset and that a deliberately
   stalled application recovers automatically.
8. Keep SWD enabled, but document how debugger halting affects the watchdog
   test and do not treat a paused debugger as proof of normal operation.

